-- claude-opus-48-dynamic-workflows: Corrected days between releases from 41 to 42, and added Opus 4.7's release date (April 16, 2026) for clarity.; Removed the unverified USAMO 2026 math score claim (69.3%→96.7%, '27-point jump') not found in Anthropic's announcement; replaced with the actual benchmarks Anthropic cited (Terminal-Bench 2.1, OSWorld-Verified, Finance Agent v2, Online-Mind2Web).; Rewrote headline and summary to drop the math-leap framing since it rested on the unverified USAMO claim.; Changed '1,000 parallel subagents' to 'hundreds of parallel subagents in a single session,' matching Anthropic's wording, and noted 
UPDATE news SET title = $t$Claude Opus 4.8 Brings Dynamic Workflows, Cheaper Fast Mode, Fewer Missed Code Flaws$t$, summary = $t$Anthropic launches Opus 4.8 on May 28, 2026, 42 days after Opus 4.7 (released April 16, 2026). Dynamic Workflows, a Claude Code research preview, runs hundreds of parallel subagents in a single session for repo-scale tasks. Fast mode drops from $30/$150 to $10/$50 per 1M tokens — a 3x cost reduction. Same base price: $5/$25 per 1M tokens. Opus 4.8 is also roughly four times less likely to let flaws in its own code pass unflagged.$t$, body = $t$<p>Anthropic launched <strong>Claude Opus 4.8</strong> on May 28, 2026 — 42 days after Opus 4.7, which was released April 16, 2026. The release is positioned as a significant quality-of-life update: faster responses, better long-context consistency, and improvements across agentic and coding benchmarks.</p>

    <h2>Benchmark improvements</h2>
    <p>Anthropic highlighted gains on Terminal-Bench 2.1, OSWorld-Verified, Finance Agent v2, and Online-Mind2Web, alongside improvements to coding reliability. Opus 4.8 is also roughly <strong>four times less likely</strong> to let flaws in its own code pass without flagging them, and proactively signals uncertainty rather than confidently hallucinating.</p>

    <h2>Dynamic Workflows in Claude Code</h2>
    <p>The biggest agentic feature is <strong>Dynamic Workflows</strong>, a research preview that lets Claude Code plan a large task, run <strong>hundreds of parallel subagents in a single session</strong>, distribute the work across them, and verify outputs against your test suite — all without human intervention. Dynamic Workflows is available for Claude Code on Enterprise, Team, and Max plans, and is designed for repository-scale migrations and hundreds-of-thousands-of-lines-of-code refactors that would previously require a team of engineers.</p>

    <h2>Pricing: same base, cheaper Fast mode</h2>
    <p>Standard pricing holds at <strong>$5 per million input tokens and $25 per million output tokens</strong> — identical to Opus 4.7. The big change is in Fast mode: it drops from <strong>$30/$150</strong> per million tokens (Opus 4.7) to <strong>$10/$50</strong> — a 3× cost reduction for latency-sensitive workloads. Prompt caching brings savings up to 90%; batch processing offers 50% off.</p>
    <p>Opus 4.8 is available in Claude.ai for Pro, Max, Team, and Enterprise subscribers, and via the Anthropic API, AWS Bedrock, Google Cloud Vertex AI, and Microsoft Foundry.</p>

    <h2>Mythos Preview expanding access</h2>
    <p>Separately, Anthropic said it expects to bring <strong>Mythos-class models</strong> — its restricted-access cybersecurity models — to all its customers "in the coming weeks." Mythos Preview has already been used by a small number of organizations: during its 30-day preview, Mythos and roughly 50 partner organizations identified more than 10,000 high- or critical-severity vulnerabilities in systemically important software.</p>
